  <h3 id="sections"><span class="secno">3.3. </span>Sections and headings</h3>

  <p class="big-issue"> This section will explain how to interpret sections
   and headers in order to obtain the correct outline view of the document.

  <h2 id="sections0"><span class="secno">4. </span>Sections</h2>

  <p class="big-issue"> These are just notes that will be collapsed into the
   previous section. (The paragraphs that have already been merged with the
   previous section have now been deleted, leaving this section somewhat
   gutted of comprehensible content.)

  <h3 id="document0"><span class="secno">4.1. </span>Document sections</h3>

  <p>User agents must support all of the common attributes and event handlers
   on the <code><a href="#section4">section</a></code> element, as well as
   the <code>active</code> attribute (for use with <span><a
   href="#mutually1">mutually exclusive sections</a></span>).

  <p>In CSS-aware user agents, the default presentation of this element
   should be achieved by including the following rules, or their equivalent,
   in the UA's user agent style sheet:

  <pre>@namespace xh url(http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml);
xh|section { display: block; margin: 1em 0; }</pre>

  <h4 id="section"><span class="secno">4.1.1. </span>Section headers</h4>

  <p>The semantics of <code><a href="#h10">h1</a></code> ... <code><a
   href="#h60">h6</a></code> elements that have ancestor <code><a
   href="#section4">section</a></code> elements are all the same: they simply
   specify the header of that section.</p>
  <!--
  <p>A section can contain multiple headers, XXX what would the second
  header mean?</p>
-->

  <p>For <code><a href="#h10">h1</a></code> elements, CSS-aware visual user
   agents should derive the size of the header from the level of <code><a
   href="#section4">section</a></code> nesting. This effect should be
   achieved by including the following rules, or their equivalent, in the
   UA's user agent style sheet:

  <pre>@namespace xh url(http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml);
xh|section xh|h1 { /* same styles as h2 */ }
xh|section xh|section xh|h1 { /* same styles as h3 */ }
xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|h1 { /* same styles as h4 */ }
xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|h1 { /* same styles as h5 */ }
xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|section xh|h1 { /* same styles as h6 */ }</pre>

  <p>Authors should use <code><a href="#h10">h1</a></code> elements to denote
   headers in sections. Authors may instead use <code><a
   href="#h20">h2</a></code> ... <code><a href="#h60">h6</a></code> elements,
   for backwards compatibility with user agents that do not support <code><a
   href="#section4">section</a></code> elements.

  <h4 id="section0"><span class="secno">4.1.2. </span>Section footers</h4>

  <p class="big-issue">Need to clarify that the <code><a
   href="#address0">address</a></code> element is purely for a section's
   contact information and <em>not</em> for any arbitrary address.</p>
